trace.content:
[THOUGHT] All eight evidence domains have now been fully and systematically collected:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — fatigue, weight loss (~1 month), jaundice (~2 weeks ago);  
2) Course — progressive, non-fluctuating, no pain or pruritus;  
3) Associated symptoms — no night sweats, lymphadenopathy, bleeding, infections, pruritus, dark urine, pale stools, RUQ/back pain;  
4) Past history — no chronic illness, no liver/pancreatic disease, no surgeries;  
5) Medications/allergies — none;  
6) Social/exposures — heavy tobacco (1 pack/day) and alcohol (35 drinks/week);  
7) Physical exam — icterus, enlarged non-tender gallbladder (Courvoisier’s sign), no hepatosplenomegaly or lymphadenopathy;  
8) Labs/imaging — elevated ALP > AST/ALT, elevated bilirubin, positive urine bilirubin, stone-free enlarged gallbladder on ultrasound.  

This constellation is highly specific for **painless obstructive jaundice due to malignant distal biliary obstruction**, most likely **pancreatic head adenocarcinoma**, given the risk profile and classic findings. No further diagnostic information can be obtained within the 16-turn limit, and all domains are complete.

[FIRST]

